  "before_interfaces": {
    "def-ip-2d-radon-transform": [
      "## Definition\n\nWrite $C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$ for the complex-valued smooth functions of compact support on the Euclidean plane. For $u\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, $n=1$ or $2$, use the compact-support Riemann Fourier transform\n$$\\widehat u(\\xi)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}u(x)e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}\\,dx.$$\nFor $\\theta=(\\theta_1,\\theta_2)\\in S^1:=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:|x|=1\\}$ put $\\theta^\\perp=(-\\theta_2,\\theta_1)$. The line with normal $\\theta$ and signed offset $s$ is $\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^2:x\\cdot\\theta=s\\}$. Its **Euclidean Radon transform** is\n$$Rf(\\theta,s):=\\int_{\\mathbb R}f(s\\theta+t\\theta^\\perp)\\,dt,\\qquad f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C),\\ (\\theta,s)\\in S^1\\times\\mathbb R.$$\nThe line integral is a compact-support Riemann integral.\nThe full data set uses every oriented normal $\\theta\\in S^1$ and every $s\\in\\mathbb R$; the same unoriented line is represented by $(\\theta,s)$ and $(-\\theta,-s)$.\nFor a one-dimensional function $h$ with integrable Fourier transform, define\n$$|D_s|h(s):=\\int_{\\mathbb R}2\\pi|\\sigma|\\,\\widehat h(\\sigma)e^{2\\pi i s\\sigma}\\,d\\sigma.$$\nFor $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$ define the Riesz potential notation used here by\n$$|D|^{-1}f(x):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}e^{2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi}\\frac{\\widehat f(\\xi)}{2\\pi|\\xi|}\\,d\\xi.$$\nThe quotient in the last integral is assigned value $0$ at $\\xi=0$. These integrals are absolutely convergent on the stated classes: compact-support integration by parts gives rapid decay of $\\widehat f$ and $\\widehat h$, while $\\widehat f$ is bounded near $0$ and $|\\xi|^{-1}$ is locally integrable in two dimensions. Here the multiplier for $|D_s|$ is $2\\pi|\\sigma|$, corresponding to the $e^{-2\\pi i s\\sigma}$ Fourier convention."
    ],
    "thm-ip-fourier-slice-identity": [
      "## Statement\n\nFor every $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$, every unit normal $\\theta\\in S^1$, and every $\\sigma\\in\\mathbb R$, the compact-support Fourier transform of the line data in the offset variable equals the restriction of the two-dimensional Fourier transform to the line in frequency space with direction $\\theta$:\n$$\\widehat{Rf}(\\theta,\\sigma)=\\widehat f(\\sigma\\theta).$$"
    ],
    "thm-ip-radon-normal-operator": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume Countable Choice. Let $\\mu$ be the polar surface measure on $S^1$, defined on Borel $E\\subseteq S^1$ by\n$$\\mu(E):=2\\lambda_2\\bigl(\\{r\\theta:\\theta\\in E,\\ 0<r\\le1\\}\\bigr),$$\nwhere $\\lambda_2$ is Lebesgue measure on $\\mathbb R^2$. For continuous $g:S^1\\times\\mathbb R\\to\\mathbb C$ for which the integral exists, define its **backprojection** by\n$$R^*g(x):=\\int_{S^1}g(\\theta,x\\cdot\\theta)\\,d\\mu(\\theta).$$\nFor every $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$ and every $x\\in\\mathbb R^2$, the normal-operator and filtered-backprojection identities hold:\n$$R^*Rf(x)=4\\pi|D|^{-1}f(x),\\qquad f(x)=\\frac1{4\\pi}R^*|D_s|Rf(x),$$\nwith $|D|^{-1}$ and $|D_s|$ as defined for the compact-support Fourier convention in the Euclidean Radon transform definition."
    ],
    "alg-ip-filtered-backprojection-complete-data": [
      "## Definition\n\nAssume Countable Choice and let $\\mu$ be the polar surface measure on $S^1$, with $\\mu(E)=2\\lambda_2(\\{r\\theta:\\theta\\in E,\\ 0<r\\le1\\})$ for Borel $E\\subseteq S^1$. The **complete-data filtered-backprojection procedure** takes as input the exact full sinogram $g=Rf$ of some $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$; all oriented normals $\\theta\\in S^1$ and all offsets $s\\in\\mathbb R$ are supplied. It returns the function $f_{\\mathrm{FBP}}:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb C$ defined by\n$$f_{\\mathrm{FBP}}(x)=\\frac1{4\\pi}\\int_{S^1}q(\\theta,x\\cdot\\theta)\\,d\\mu(\\theta),$$\nwhere the filter output is\n$$q(\\theta,s)=\\int_{\\mathbb R}2\\pi|\\sigma|\\,\\widehat{g(\\theta,\\cdot)}(\\sigma)e^{2\\pi i s\\sigma}\\,d\\sigma.$$\nThe exact instructions are: (1) for each $\\theta$, take the one-dimensional Fourier transform of $g(\\theta,\\cdot)$ with the negative-sign $2\\pi$ convention; (2) multiply that transform by the declared ramp filter $2\\pi|\\sigma|$ and take its inverse Fourier integral to form $q(\\theta,\\cdot)$; (3) evaluate $q(\\theta,\\cdot)$ at $s=x\\cdot\\theta$ and integrate over the full polar surface measure in $\\theta$; (4) divide by $4\\pi$. For this input class the slice identity and Schwartz decay make the filter integral absolutely convergent and the backprojection integral well defined. This definition specifies the continuous full-data procedure; it gives no guarantee for finitely sampled, truncated, or noisy measurements. Exact recovery on the stated class is proved by the normal-operator theorem."
    ],
